+# Use a disposable catalog cache
+
+For whoever changes catalog reads or operates the server. Catalog query results may live in Redis as disposable
+copies of Postgres data, because the pantry item page is slow on Saturday mornings.
+
+## Context
+
+The item page was reported slow on Saturday mornings, and Redis was requested in front of its query, with Tom
+available to run it on the server. The repository has no timings or query plans identifying the bottleneck.
+Earlier decisions kept sessions and jobs in Postgres to reduce operational work; Redis previously lost queued
+receipts, as [the job decision](0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) records. This cache must tolerate eviction
+and outages, bound stock staleness, and leave authoritative data in Postgres.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Continue querying Postgres for every search**: adds no service and always reads current data; leaves repeated
+  searches doing the same query during the reported peak. Query tuning remains possible once plans are available.
+- **Cache in each web process**: adds no service, but each process fills and holds its own copy rather than sharing
+  results between servers.
+- **A shared disposable cache**: chosen, using the requested Redis service that Tom can operate.
+
+## Decision
+
+Cache catalog search results in Redis with a short expiry and fall back to Postgres on cache failure. Redis is
+optional: it is enabled through the [catalog configuration](../features/catalog.md#configuration).
+Sessions and background jobs continue to use Postgres under their existing decisions.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Repeated searches can skip the database query. Cached stock can lag behind Postgres; the
+[catalog limits](../features/catalog.md#known-limits) describe this trade-off. Redis adds a service to operate,
+but losing its contents loses no authoritative data. Tom's availability supplies an operator, not evidence of
+additional operational coverage. Peak latency and cache hit rates still need measurement on the server.
